<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  Not the launcher's application manifest. This one is put in the executable
  under resource id 2 and switched on by hand, for the moment the update
  question is on the screen and for nothing else.

  It is here for one reason: that question is asked with a task dialog, the
  kind with a heading and command links rather than three cryptic buttons, and
  TaskDialogIndirect exists only in version 6 of the common controls. Without
  something declaring that dependency, a program is handed version 5, the entry
  point is simply not there, and the dialog would quietly fall back to a
  message box on every machine rather than on none.

  Why not the ordinary way, as the application manifest under id 1: MinGW's
  linker puts its own manifest there for every executable it builds, and a
  second one at the same id is not merged but rejected, with ".rsrc merge
  failure: multiple non-default manifests". Both then end up in the file.
  Nothing here is worth breaking every build over, and the activation context
  is the documented way to ask for version 6 without touching the manifest the
  toolchain already wrote. See ask() in launcher.c.

  Nothing about privileges is declared here on purpose. That belongs in the
  application manifest, the linker's own already says asInvoker, and that is
  the right answer: a program that replaces a file next to HLSW must not
  quietly ask for administrator rights. If the folder is not writable, the
  update says so and changes nothing.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <assemblyIdentity type="win32" name="hlswfix.taskdialog" version="1.0.0.0"
                    processorArchitecture="*"/>
  <dependency>
    <dependentAssembly>
      <assemblyIdentity type="win32" name="Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls"
                        version="6.0.0.0" processorArchitecture="*"
                        publicKeyToken="6595b64144ccf1df" language="*"/>
    </dependentAssembly>
  </dependency>
</assembly>
